<div class="wrap">
<header class="top">
<div class="label">S-MAD resubmission · competing detectors under construction controls · prepared for Paweł Narolski and co-author</div>
<h1>Detectors without construction shortcuts</h1>
<p class="lede">You asked two things. First, how the competing detectors do on regular (landmark), GAN and diffusion morphs once the benchmarks' construction shortcuts are taken away. Second, for each method, which shortcut was found, and what it would take to show it beyond doubt. This page joins the two answers. Section 2 is the comparison table; section 3 is the shortcut-per-method table; section 4 says what is still missing.</p>
<div class="facts">
<span class="chip k-asof">As of 2026-11-07, 18:47 CET</span>
<span class="chip k-seal">MADMamba sealed, not shown</span>
<span class="chip k-read">A42 amendment 1: read, post hoc</span>
<span class="chip k-pend">A43: pending</span>
</div>
</header>
<div class="prose"><p><strong>Scope</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>One network per release.</strong> The official releases are the authors' weights, one network each. Their claims are about those checkpoints, not the SelfMAD or MADation methods.</li>
<li><strong>Our frames.</strong> Releases are scored on our 512-px eye-aligned frames unless a row says otherwise. Only the SelfMAD release was also scored with its own crop on the raw files.</li>
<li><strong>Recipes are our re-implementations</strong>, trained on SMDD only; most did not reproduce the published numbers.</li>
<li><strong>MADMamba is sealed</strong> and not on this page ("MADMamba not evaluated on H3 (sealed)"). No W3, E10 or FST number is used, and no <code>fst*</code> file, <code>manifests/valx.csv</code> or <code>A12SP</code> path was opened.</li>
<li><strong>Wording awaits the author's sign-off</strong> (Sun 8 Nov 12:00), which can only drop or weaken it.</li>
</ul></div>
<nav class="toc" aria-label="Sections">
<a href="#s1"><b>1</b>What "without shortcuts" means</a>
<a href="#s2"><b>2</b>Comparison table</a>
<a href="#s3"><b>3</b>Shortcut per method</a>
<a href="#s4"><b>4</b>What would make it beyond doubt</a>
<a href="#s5"><b>5</b>What this does not show</a>
<a href="#sources">Sources</a>
</nav>
<section class="s" id="s1">
<h2><span class="n">1</span>What "without shortcuts" means, and how it was tested</h2>
<div class="prose"><p><strong>The problem.</strong> A morph detector has to tell morphs (one face image built from two people) from genuine photos of one person. On the standard benchmarks the two classes were also made in different ways:</p>
<ul>
<li>The genuine photos are large, high-quality camera JPEG files.</li>
<li>The morphs are files of other sizes and formats. In most families their faces are also smaller. The exceptions are MAD22 OpenCV and Webmorph and FRLL StyleGAN, where the face sizes already match.</li>
<li>Several landmark families also carry black alignment borders. Border fraction alone separates several evaluation families almost perfectly (AUC 0.99–1.00; E07 README).</li>
<li>GAN and diffusion morphs are generator output, while the genuine photos come from a camera.</li>
<li><strong>Claim B.</strong> In ten registered cells (the seven FRLL diffusion families and MAD22 MIPGAN-I, MIPGAN-II and FaceMorpher), one documented file attribute alone separates morphs from genuine photos: "Morph and bona fide files differ in documented construction attributes that alone separate the classes (O = 1.000 per decision cell)."</li>
</ul>
<p>FRLL is the Face Research Lab London set; MAD22 is the SYN-MAD 2022 benchmark. The networks never see file headers. They see decoded pixels, so file attributes matter only through what they leave in the pixels.</p>
<p>So a detector can score well by noticing how the file or picture was made, without noticing that two identities were blended. This page calls such a difference a <strong>construction difference</strong>. The word <strong>shortcut</strong> is kept for A41's registered definition, which so far applies to C1 only. Under it, three things must all hold:</p>
<ol>
<li>A property separates the classes.</li>
<li>The property can be put into genuine photos without morphing.</li>
<li>A control that removes it removes at least half of the separation.</li>
</ol>
<p>No control removes every difference: E7 leaves the borders, and H1 leaves file size. To see what a detector does without a given difference, we either <strong>change the genuine side</strong> so that it shares one construction property with the morphs (face size in E7, the generator family in X3), or <strong>build a new set</strong> in which both classes are made the same way and only the number of identities differs (H1, H2, H3, H4b). Then we score the same networks again.</p></div><div class="prose"><h3>The two kinds of number</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Equal error rate (EER, %)</strong> is thresholded: the error rate where missed morphs and false alarms are equally frequent. 0 is perfect and 50 is chance. Above 50 means the detector rates the genuine images as more morph-like than the morphs ("inverted"). The threshold is re-chosen for each comparison, so EER does not show how a deployed detector behaves; the flag rates at a fixed threshold do.</li>
<li><strong>Area under the curve (AUC)</strong> is threshold-free: the chance that a random morph scores above a random comparison image. 0.5 is chance; below 0.5 means the comparison images look more morph-like than the morphs.</li>
<li><strong>Floors.</strong> Some controls have a floor: the AUC a simple statistic (file size, eye position, or a combination) reaches on the same comparison. A detector shows something beyond that statistic only if it clearly beats the floor.</li>
</ul></div><div class="tw"><table class="small">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Control</th>
<th>Floors (AUC)</th>
<th>Rule</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>H1</td>
<td>bytes 0.620 (<code>orig</code>) / 0.681 (<code>pp</code>); eye geometry 0.526 / 0.522</td>
<td>A36 amendment 1 label rule ([b])</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>H3</td>
<td>bytes 0.526; eye geometry 0.560; combined covariates 0.607–0.617</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>H4b</td>
<td>bytes 0.912</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>H2</td>
<td>bytes 0.522; eye geometry 0.518</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Arc2Face</td>
<td>bytes 0.514; eye geometry 0.509</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>X4</td>
<td>none logged (only a kill rule)</td>
<td></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>X3</td>
<td>cue floors are print-only (Q-G2)</td>
<td>not used for any statement here</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div><div class="prose"><h3>How each morph type was tested</h3></div><div class="cols"><div><h3>Regular (landmark) morphs</h3><ul>
<li><strong>Shrunk genuine photos (E7).</strong> Each genuine photo is shrunk (area averaging) to the family's median morph face size before the same alignment: ~1.0x for MAD22 OpenCV and Webmorph, 3.3x for MAD22 FaceMorpher. This matches face size only, not processing history, and the alignment borders remain.</li>
<li><strong>The controlled Poisson set (H1).</strong> A fifth landmark tool (MediaPipe Delaunay warp plus Poisson blend) on CASIA-WebFace identities at 112 px; 598 pairs. Both classes share source photos, size, the ~40% black border and post-processing. The only known remaining difference is file size (morphs are 3–5% smaller PNGs): floor 0.620 on <code>orig</code> and 0.681 on <code>pp</code>. <code>orig</code> is the registered primary variant.</li>
</ul></div><div><h3>GAN morphs</h3><ul>
<li><strong>H3.</strong> StyleGAN3 two-person latent-average morphs against single-person renders of the same generator. Its classes differ in latent spread by construction.</li>
<li><strong>X4.</strong> The same morphs against renders matched to the morphs' latent spread; little power against a two-identity signal by construction.</li>
<li><strong>H4b.</strong> StyleGAN v1 MIPGAN-I-style morphs against single-identity renders of the host; file size alone gives 0.912.</li>
<li><strong>Renders vs camera (H-new-83).</strong> A separate check of whether a detector scores generator output above camera photos. Registered verdict "neither"; per-pool AUCs descriptive.</li>
</ul></div><div><h3>Diffusion morphs</h3><ul>
<li><strong>Shrunk genuine photos (E7).</strong> Each diffusion family has its own dose; the DiM pools average 3.19x. For the SelfMAD release it was also run on the raw files with its own crop, with three shrinking methods: one result, not three replications.</li>
<li><strong>Genuine photos through the morphs' generator family (X3).</strong> DiffAE reconstructions of the genuine photos, photos only resized by DiffAE's aligner, and one-person "morphs". Releases only. Its positive control failed (FE ViT-L/14 0.636 ≤ 0.65): "assay sensitivity not shown".</li>
<li><strong>Other diffusion generators (H2, Arc2Face).</strong> H2 is low-potency (1.1% of morphs verify against both contributors); Arc2Face is the potent set (94.7%). Both columns are descriptive.</li>
</ul></div></div>
</section>
<section class="s" id="s2">
<h2><span class="n">2</span>Comparison table: regular, GAN and diffusion morphs under the controls</h2>
<div class="prose"><h3>How to read the table</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Cells.</strong> "→" joins a benchmark number and its controlled number of the same metric (EER → EER). " | " separates different tests or metrics.</li>
<li><strong>Rows.</strong> Releases are the authors' official weights, one network each. Recipes are our re-implementations, trained on SMDD only (d0); their numbers are means over the stated number of networks (seeds).</li>
<li><strong>‡</strong> marks the 12 primary rows of the registered GAN test (C3). Rows 4–8 are descriptive and outside C3.</li>
<li><strong>Verdict words</strong> ("holds", "partly holds", "does not hold", "not shown") are post hoc labels of this note, not A41 grades. "Partly holds" is not the dropped phrase "partly carried".</li>
</ul></div><div class="tw"><table class="cmp">
<thead>
<tr>
<th>#</th>
<th>Method (networks)</th>
<th>Regular (landmark) morphs [a][b]</th>
<th>GAN morphs [c][d]</th>
<th>Diffusion morphs [e][f]</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="grp"><td colspan="5">Official releases</td></tr>
<tr>
<td>1</td>
<td>SelfMAD release, HRNet-W18, its own dlib crop and resize on our frames (1) ‡</td>
<td>EER FM/OCV/AMSL/WM 0.4/0.1/2.9/5.9 → 4.8/3.9/33.3/51.9 (WM above 50 under E7); MAD22 FM 0.0 → 0.11 | H1 <code>orig</code> 0.872 (point; above in A39's count, descriptive) · <code>pp</code> 0.821 (point, no label). <span class="chip v-part">partly holds</span></td>
<td>MIPGAN-I EER 0.5 | H3 0.353 (reversed) | X4 0.447 | H4b 0.405. <span class="chip v-ns">not shown</span></td>
<td>EER Greedy-DiM 8.4 → 90.7, MorDIFF 2.5 → 61.3 (C1; inverted) | X3 rec 0.024 / 0.330 (reversed) | H2 0.510. <span class="chip v-no">does not hold</span></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2</td>
<td>MADation release, CLIP ViT-B/16 + LoRA (1) ‡</td>
<td>EER 2.8/2.9/15.1/4.9 → 4.4/4.4/28.9/10.8; MAD22 FM 0.6 → 5.9 | H1 <code>orig</code> 0.770 (above) · <code>pp</code> 0.727 (not above). <span class="chip v-part">partly holds</span></td>
<td>MIPGAN-I EER 18.4; G-test 9.9 | H3 0.554 | X4 0.423 | H4b 0.716. <span class="chip v-ns">not shown</span></td>
<td>EER MorDIFF 4.9 → 19.6 (C2), DiM-C 6.4 → 29.5, DiM-A 20.6 → 56.4, Greedy-DiM 54.0 → 84.9 | X3 rec MorDIFF 0.847 (open), DiM-A 0.361 | H2 0.497. <span class="chip v-no">does not hold</span></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>3</td>
<td>MADation release, CLIP ViT-L/14 + LoRA (1) ‡</td>
<td>EER 1.0/1.0/17.7/4.9 → 1.0/1.0/14.7/4.4; MAD22 FM 0.0 → 0.55 | H1 <code>orig</code> 0.718 (above) · <code>pp</code> 0.684 (not above). <span class="chip v-yes">holds</span></td>
<td>MIPGAN-I EER 11.9; G-test 69.5 (inverted) | H3 0.427 (reversed) | X4 0.445 | H4b 0.902. <span class="chip v-ns">not shown</span></td>
<td>EER Greedy-DiM 9.8 → 9.8, DiM-A 22.0 → 16.7, MorDIFF 28.9 → 39.7 | X3 rec Greedy-DiM 0.749 (floor-limited) | H2 0.466. <span class="chip v-part">partly holds</span></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>4</td>
<td>MADation baseline FE ViT-B/16, frozen CLIP + head (1)</td>
<td>EER 16.7/18.6/26.0/22.5 → 22.6/26.0/38.7/31.9; MAD22 FM 1.0 → 7.2 | H1 <code>orig</code> 0.681 (above, descriptive) · <code>pp</code> 0.681 (not above). <span class="chip v-part">partly holds</span></td>
<td>MIPGAN-I EER 35.1; G-test 35.1 | H3 0.514 | X4 0.451 | H4b 0.661. <span class="chip v-ns">not shown</span></td>
<td>EER DiM 21.0–47.0 → 41.6–73.6, MorDIFF 39.7 → 53.5 | X3 not run | H2 0.498. <span class="chip v-no">does not hold</span></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>5</td>
<td>MADation baseline FE ViT-L/14, frozen CLIP + head (1)</td>
<td>EER 3.9/4.4/21.6/9.8 → 4.4/4.9/23.0/10.3; MAD22 FM 1.6 → 2.7 | H1 <code>orig</code> 0.735 · <code>pp</code> 0.745 (both above, descriptive). <span class="chip v-yes">holds</span></td>
<td>MIPGAN-I EER 8.5; G-test 29.1 | H3 0.505 | X4 0.475 | H4b 0.804. <span class="chip v-ns">not shown</span></td>
<td>EER Greedy-DiM-S 11.8 → 13.8, Greedy-DiM 22.0 → 28.0, MorDIFF 34.8 → 32.4 | X3 rec Greedy-DiM 0.636 (positive-control gate failed), DiM-A 0.772, MorDIFF 0.742 | H2 0.502. <span class="chip v-part">partly holds</span></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>6</td>
