</table></div><div class="prose"><h3>Notes to the table</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Confidence-interval levels.</strong><ul>
<li>Person-clustered 95%: the A41 cells (S2, S3, DC-8, X3, X7′) and A42 (border-matched and central-window X3, the central-window rises).</li>
<li>Pair 90%: the A36 H1 and H3 values.</li>
<li>TOST (90%, over seeds plus the image bootstrap): the A12-BR reproduction intervals (row 11).</li>
<li>Seed means, or mean ± SD: the recipes.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>H1 labels.</strong> Registered for the 11 primary rows (M1–M8O and the ViT-B/16 and ViT-L/14 releases), with no grade and set post-freeze. The SelfMAD own-crop H1 (<code>orig</code> 0.872) is a point estimate, counted "above" only in A39's sensitivity read.</li>
<li><strong>No landmark (regular) cell has a shortcut grade.</strong> E7 leaves the alignment borders on landmark families (E07 README), and border fraction alone separates several evaluation families almost perfectly (AUC 0.99–1.00). On MorDIFF, 0.471 of the pool's difference from the native photos sits in the warped border (H-new-152 W-c).</li>
<li><strong>Recipes.</strong> No registered shortcut rule was applied to them. A12.4 calls their benchmark pool "an eval-pipeline diagnostic, never a detection result".</li>
<li><strong>Printed beside every claim in the paper</strong> (CONCLUSIONS):<ul>
<li>the positive controls: the X3 gate failed (0.636 ≤ 0.65), and the ViT-L/14 release is unchanged on Greedy-DiM (Δ 0.00 [−3.15, 3.41]);</li>
<li>our own networks' history with the same shortcut (take it from CONCLUSIONS);</li>
<li>"MADMamba not evaluated on H3 (sealed)".</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul></div><div class="prose"><h3>Method by method: what was found, how we know, how sure</h3></div><div class="cards"><article class="card"><h3><span class="n">1</span>SelfMAD release</h3><ul>
<li><em>What:</em> meets A41's shortcut definition on diffusion morphs (C1). It treats genuine photos that were only shrunk to the morphs' face size as more morph-like than real Greedy-DiM and MorDIFF morphs. Cue: "bona fide processing history; cue not identified".</li>
<li><em>How we know:</em><ul>
<li>Against photos shrunk to the morphs' face size before the same alignment (E7), its Greedy-DiM EER goes 8.4% → 90.7% (+82.4 [77.3, 87.4]).</li>
<li>The same control leaves MAD22 FaceMorpher morphs detected (0.11%) and leaves the ViT-L/14 release unchanged on Greedy-DiM (Δ 0.00).</li>
<li>With its own crop on the raw files, its published regime reproduces (6.4% against 7.6%) and our frames do not change the native regime. There the reversal persists (6.4% → 87.2%, descriptive).</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>
<p><em>How sure:</em> this is C1, graded "strong (final)", the highest grade any shortcut claim reached here, but not the top grade. Two registered conditions are open:</p>
<ul>
<li>the reconstruction test's positive control failed (0.636 ≤ 0.65);</li>
<li>a border or alignment artefact of our control photos is not formally excluded.</li>
</ul>
<p>It is also one checkpoint on 204 photos of 102 persons, the earlier registered test said "neither", and it says nothing about the SelfMAD method.</p>
</li>
</ul></article><article class="card"><h3><span class="n">2</span>MADation release ViT-B/16</h3><ul>
<li><em>What:</em> no shortcut is shown; a registered refuter fired. On MorDIFF its EER rises against shrunk photos (C2's registered cue word "fine-detail softness"; cue not identified), but our rules do not let us call this a shortcut.</li>
<li><em>How we know:</em><ul>
<li>The rise, 4.9% → 19.6% (+14.7 [10.3, 18.9]), appears in all five crops (+13.2 to +21.0).</li>
<li>It removes less than half of the separation (d′ share 0.46). Tier 2 is not reached (p10 0.0170 &gt; 0.00833). The same control also moves the ViT-L/14 release (+10.7).</li>
<li>The release separates the published MorDIFF morphs from DiffAE reconstructions (0.85 [0.80, 0.89]), a registered refuter.</li>
<li>Two DiM variants also rise, from 6–7% to 29% (DiM-C, Greedy-DiM-S; descriptive, not decision cells).</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><em>How sure:</em> this is C2, a "descriptive row (final)": "Identity evidence or a file/frame property: open." A42 amendment 1 adds only the explanation sentence beside C2. A43 (the MorDIFF two-by-two, H-new-241; staged, not registered) is pending.</li>
</ul></article><article class="card"><h3><span class="n">3</span>MADation release ViT-L/14</h3><ul>
<li><em>What:</em> the negative control. It is the detector that ignores downsampling on Greedy-DiM and photo-only; its MorDIFF pool rise is +10.7.</li>
<li><em>How we know:</em> its Greedy-DiM EER is unchanged under the control (9.8 → 9.8, Δ 0.00 [−3.15, 3.41]), and shrunk photos do not look more morph-like to it (0.494 → 0.455). It does react to JPEG-50 photos (52.9% flagged).</li>
<li><em>How sure:</em> it is registered as a control component (F4 (iii) supported), not as a claim about the detector. On GAN morphs it does not rank morphs above single-person renders by the registered margin (H3 0.427, reversed; C3).</li>
</ul></article><article class="card"><h3><span class="n">4</span>FE ViT-B/16</h3><ul>
<li><em>What:</em> not tested under a registered rule.</li>
<li><em>How we know:</em> its numbers fall under E7 on landmark and DiM morphs, but most of them start above 10%. No registered rule or person interval was applied, and X3 was not run.</li>
<li><em>How sure:</em> descriptive only, one network.</li>
</ul></article><article class="card"><h3><span class="n">5</span>FE ViT-L/14</h3><ul>
<li><em>What:</em> not tested under a registered rule.</li>
<li><em>How we know:</em> it changes little under E7 (Greedy-DiM-S +1.98 [0.25, 4.72]). It separates published MorDIFF morphs from reconstructions (0.742 [0.689, 0.792]), which is not shown to be identity evidence (no fidelity check covered MorDIFF).</li>
<li><em>How sure:</em> descriptive. As the registered positive control on Greedy-DiM it reached only 0.636 ≤ 0.65. That failure is why C1 cannot meet N3. It does not void C2's refuter, which fired under its own registered rule.</li>
</ul></article><article class="card"><h3><span class="n">6</span>FS ViT-B/16</h3><ul>
<li><em>What:</em> not tested under a registered rule.</li>
<li><em>How we know:</em> it is weak natively. Its DiM numbers (16–24%) go to 42–46% against shrunk photos, but they start above A41's 10% entry bar.</li>
<li><em>How sure:</em> descriptive only, one network.</li>
</ul></article><article class="card"><h3><span class="n">7</span>FS ViT-L/14</h3><ul>
<li><em>What:</em> no native EER ≤ 10% to carry.</li>
<li><em>How we know:</em> it sits at 27.6–49.0% with or without the control, so there is no good result for a cue to carry. On our frames its MorDIFF EER is 43.0% against a published 40.92% (no reproduction label logged).</li>
<li><em>How sure:</em> descriptive only.</li>
</ul></article><article class="card"><h3><span class="n">8</span>MixFaceNet-MAD release</h3><ul>
<li><em>What:</em> no native EER ≤ 10% to carry.</li>
<li><em>How we know:</em> it does not reproduce on our frames (41.7 / 76.5 against the published 13.69 / 8.96). Its loader is verified on its own training files (0.45%), so the cause is our frames or the genuine-photo source, and these are not separated.</li>
<li><em>How sure:</em> descriptive only. No sentence may tie its numbers to image size.</li>
</ul></article><article class="card"><h3><span class="n">9</span>M1 Inception-MAD</h3><ul>
<li><em>What:</em> not tested under a registered rule. Descriptively, its rankings follow the benchmark's morph files, and its fair results collapse once the photos are shrunk.</li>
<li><em>How we know:</em> OpenCV-tool morphs score 3.3% from MAD22's 1351-px files and 53.6% from FRLL's 431-px files. Under E7, MAD22 FaceMorpher goes 6.4% → 39.0% and UA8c 14.4 → 49.4.</li>
<li><em>How sure:</em> descriptive only. These are seed means of a re-implementation that did not reproduce, with no person interval, no negative control and no X3.</li>
</ul></article><article class="card"><h3><span class="n">10</span>M2 MorphHRNet-style</h3><ul>
<li><em>What:</em> no native EER ≤ 10% to carry.</li>
<li><em>How we know:</em> it is inverted natively on FRLL landmark morphs (67–91%), and degrading both classes does not remove that.</li>
<li><em>How sure:</em> descriptive. On GAN morphs its 0.591 is the best H3 row and still below the combined file-size and eye-geometry floor (C3). Single networks reach a within-pair θ of 0.66, and simple image properties alone reach 0.62.</li>
</ul></article><article class="card"><h3><span class="n">11</span>M3 MADation recipe ViT-B/16</h3><ul>
<li><em>What:</em> not tested under a registered rule. On regular and diffusion morphs it changes little under E7 (seed means, descriptive).</li>
<li><em>How we know:</em> on GAN morphs:<ul>
<li>its 7.0% MIPGAN-I EER compares generator output with camera photos;</li>
<li>it scores single-person renders above camera photos (0.951 / 0.956 / 0.967; renders vs camera, registered only for the FRLL pool, H-new-83 "neither");</li>
<li>with both classes from the generator, it ranks renders above morphs (H3 0.448).</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><em>How sure:</em> the GAN absence is C3, "strong (narrow)". The renders-vs-camera numbers are descriptive, and no causal GAN test has run (X6).<ul>
<li>Allowed: "its GAN benchmark number is not evidence of morph detection".</li>
<li>Not allowed: "it uses a GAN shortcut".</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul></article><article class="card"><h3><span class="n">12</span>M3L MADation recipe ViT-L/14</h3><ul>
<li><em>What:</em> not tested under a registered rule; the pattern is the same as M3.</li>
<li><em>How we know:</em> MIPGAN-I is 0.8% on synthetic vs camera, H3 is 0.401 (reversed), and H4b is not above the file-size floor (0.923 against 0.912; Bonferroni lower bound −0.031).</li>
<li><em>How sure:</em> C3 "strong (narrow)" for the absence; the rest is descriptive.</li>
</ul></article><article class="card"><h3><span class="n">13</span>M4 SelfMAD-style</h3><ul>
<li><em>What:</em> no native EER ≤ 10% to carry.</li>
<li><em>How we know:</em> it is inverted on the benchmarks. Shrinking makes photos look genuine to it (MAD22 FaceMorpher 99.8 → 0.8), the opposite of the SelfMAD release. It is above the floor on the clean landmark set (H1 0.761).</li>
<li><em>How sure:</em> descriptive only.</li>
</ul></article><article class="card"><h3><span class="n">14</span>M5 PW-MAD</h3><ul>
<li><em>What:</em> not tested under a registered rule. Descriptively, its fair native results fall once the photos are shrunk.</li>
<li><em>How we know:</em> WebMorph goes 4.2 → 14.3, MAD22 FaceMorpher 1.2 → 20.7 and UA8c 28.0 → 49.5, while the families shrunk only ~1.0x do not change.</li>
<li><em>How sure:</em> descriptive only. These are seed means with seed SD up to ±17, from a re-implementation that did not reproduce.</li>
</ul></article><article class="card"><h3><span class="n">15</span>M6 OrthoMAD</h3><ul>
<li><em>What:</em> no native EER ≤ 10% that E7 changes.</li>
<li><em>How we know:</em> it is inverted natively on FRLL landmark morphs (59.8–81.4%), and degrading both classes does not remove that. MAD22 FaceMorpher goes 24.4 → 47.5 under E7 (descriptive). Its only native cell at or below 10%, MAD22 OpenCV (9.4 → 10.0), is shrunk only ~1.0x, so E7 tests nothing there.</li>
<li><em>How sure:</em> descriptive only.</li>
</ul></article><article class="card"><h3><span class="n">16</span>M8 MixFaceNet-MAD recipe</h3><ul>
<li><em>What:</em> no native EER ≤ 10% that E7 changes.</li>
<li><em>How we know:</em> it is weak or inverted natively. Its MAD22 FaceMorpher fall (27.6 → 65.7) starts above 10%. Its only native cell at or below 10%, MAD22 OpenCV (6.4 → 6.8), is shrunk only ~1.0x.</li>
<li><em>How sure:</em> descriptive only.</li>
</ul></article><article class="card"><h3><span class="n">17</span>M8O MixFaceNet-MAD, official code</h3><ul>
<li><em>What:</em> no native EER ≤ 10% that E7 changes.</li>
<li><em>How we know:</em> it is inverted natively on all four FRLL landmark families. Shrunk photos move toward "morph" for it (26.7% of half-size photos flagged at its native threshold), but there is no good native result for that to carry. Its MAD22 OpenCV cell (6.1 → 6.7) is shrunk only ~1.0x.</li>
<li><em>How sure:</em> descriptive only. The official-code reproduction was falsified (MAD22-5 35.78 ± 3.66, FRLL-5 67.59 ± 7.31, against 13.69 / 8.96).</li>
</ul></article></div><div class="callout"><p><strong>Across rows.</strong></p><ul>
<li>C1 is the only claim whose numbers meet A41's shortcut definition. It is graded "strong (final)"; two registered conditions are open (assay sensitivity of X3; pool artefact not formally excluded). In plain words (CONCLUSIONS): the SelfMAD release treats genuine photos that were merely shrunk to the morphs' face size as more morph-like than real Greedy-DiM morphs.</li>
<li>Every other row carries one of the fixed tags:<ul>
<li>registered refuter fired (C2, the ViT-B/16 release on MorDIFF);</li>
<li>negative control (the ViT-L/14 release);</li>
<li>not tested under a registered rule (descriptive moves under a control);</li>
<li>no native EER ≤ 10% to carry.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>Separately, there are absences on clean tests: C3 for GAN morphs, and the H1 labels for landmark morphs (registered, no grade). Neither is a shortcut grade.</li>
<li>Never write "published detectors rely on shortcuts", "for every method" or "MADation carries the shortcut".</li>
</ul></div>
</section>
<section class="s" id="s4">
<h2><span class="n">4</span>What would make it beyond doubt</h2>
<div class="prose"><p>This section names what is still missing for each claim. None of it can change a grade.</p>
<p><strong>Even the top grade would not be "beyond doubt":</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Each claim concerns one released network.</li>
<li>It is scored on one pool of 204 FRLL photos of 102 persons, and the re-runs re-use the same persons and images.</li>
<li>A41's rules were written after earlier estimates of the same questions had been seen. Those earlier registered tests said "neither".</li>
<li>The cue itself is not identified.</li>
</ul>
<p>"Beyond doubt" would need the top grade plus replication: another checkpoint or retrain, fresh data and another studio pool, all under rules fixed before the data are seen.</p></div><div class="status"><p><strong>A42 amendment 1 and A43: status</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>A42 amendment 1</strong> (the window test; the ViT-B/16 release; MorDIFF).<ul>
<li>The window was read at 18:08 CET. Its results bullet and post-result review were logged at 18:30 CET (PROTOCOL l.17322 and l.17398, commit d03dfe7), after this note's first sources were read.</li>
<li>The author answered question (i) at about 18:35 CET (logged 18:39 CET, 756dfad): his crop-slicing approval covers the central 256-px window. Under amendment 1's timing rule this changes no label.</li>
<li>A42 is post hoc and descriptive. It changes no A41 verdict, kill or grade.</li>
<li>This note uses only CONCLUSIONS' "Beside C2" sentence (quoted in §4, C2 item 1). It does not use the registered H-new-240 (b) label "held": the post-result review says "held" means the separation does not survive the central window, not that the border explains it.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>A43 (the MorDIFF two-by-two, H-new-241; staged, not registered): pending.</strong><ul>
<li>It compares the published MorDIFF morphs with our same-pair morphs, and our two-identity morphs with our one-identity morphs.</li>
<li>It is staged on branch a43 (code 8a6219e, ledger 58c5b82, 18:30 CET). No image has been generated and nothing has been scored. Nothing is predicted here.</li>
<li><strong>Name clash.</strong> Main PROTOCOL (l.17047, l.17469) and CONCLUSIONS (l.283) use "A43" for a different item: one zero-GPU registration of H-new-257, 258, 260 and 264–266. CONCLUSIONS lists the same-pair morphs (H-new-241) as "after the deadline".</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul></div><div class="prose"><p>A41's grades are final. CONCLUSIONS: "What can still change a grade. Nothing". That covers C1–C4 and B. Each item below can only change an explanation sentence, or support a new claim registered separately before its data are seen.</p>
<p><strong>C1 (SelfMAD release, diffusion morphs)</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>N3 with a working positive control.</strong> This is the one registered condition still missing. It needs a same-generator test whose positive-control detector clears its bar. On X3, FE ViT-L/14 reached only 0.636 ≤ 0.65, where simple image properties alone give 0.993. This note's sources name no scheduled item for it.</li>
<li><strong>Exclude the control-pool artefact.</strong><ul>
<li>A successor border test, with its cut set relative to the untrained-network baseline (A41's calibration note).</li>
<li>Path attribution (H-new-261, about 0.05 GPU-h, after the deadline).</li>
<li>The best-aligned-third check (H-new-260, zero GPU, proposed) can add a sentence. It cannot remove the qualifier.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>A second checkpoint, or a retrain over seeds</strong> (after the deadline). This is the SelfMAD retrain whose self-morphs use an integer shift instead of an interpolated zoom (H-new-242; 2 seeds per arm, 40–80 GPU-h). It would add retrained networks (ours, not the release) and test the mechanism note, which is "consistent with" C1 and does not explain it.</li>
<li><strong>Fresh data and another studio pool (X8)</strong> (after the deadline). Every C1 number sits on the same 204 FRLL photos of 102 persons.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>C2 (ViT-B/16 release, MorDIFF)</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>A42 amendment 1, the window test</strong> (H-new-240 (b), H-new-255): read. It added the Beside-C2 explanation sentence only, and the zoom confound stays (H-new-262/263, proposed). CONCLUSIONS, "Beside C2" (explanation, not part of "What we may write"): "Post hoc (A42; one network, the same 204 photos; the window covered by the author's ruling on (i) of 2026-11-07 about 18:35 CET): matching border area leaves the separation at 0.84. In the central 256-px window, which holds no zero-fill and which the loader enlarges about 2x, it falls to 0.61 [0.56, 0.65], while the rise against downsampled photos grows to +32.9 [28.9, 37.7]. Most of the refuter lies outside the central window or depends on the face's scale (not separated); the rise does not need the zero-filled border. Identity evidence or a file/frame property: open." Never "the kill is spurious"; never call this window a "border test".</li>
<li><strong>Same-pair MorDIFF morphs (H-new-241), staged as A43, the MorDIFF two-by-two:</strong> pending.<ul>
<li>It compares the published morphs with our same-pair morphs (how the published files were made).</li>
<li>It also compares our two-identity morphs with our one-identity morphs (identity evidence).</li>
<li>It is a staged test designed to tell identity evidence from how the published MorDIFF files were made; PROTOCOL (l.17049–17050) calls H-new-241 "the only identity-vs-pipeline test for C2". It has not been registered or run, and nothing is predicted.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Zero-GPU matching checks</strong> (H-new-257, H-new-258; proposed, not registered): is the border slope a property of the control photos, and does matching on border area and on file size per content pixel remove the separation? PROTOCOL (l.17047, l.17469) and CONCLUSIONS (l.283) plan these, with H-new-260 and 264–266, as one zero-GPU registration that they also call "A43". That is a different item from the staged MorDIFF two-by-two in item 2.</li>
<li><strong>After the deadline:</strong><ul>
<li>a fresh-data replication of the ViT-B/16 rise (H-new-234);</li>
<li>a same-scale window that keeps the zero-fill;</li>
<li>another studio pool (X8).</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>C3 (GAN morphs, absence)</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>A clean set with power against a two-identity signal and a positive control that passes.</strong> X4 sits near 0.5 by construction, and H3's classes differ in latent spread.</li>
<li><strong>Other generators.</strong> C3 covers StyleGAN3 only.</li>
<li><strong>X6, the causal test of C4</strong> (3–5 H100-h or 8–16 GB10-h, after the deadline). C4's grade is final; X6 could only support a separately registered causal claim about what a detector uses on MIPGAN-I/II.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Regular (landmark) morphs (no shortcut grade registered)</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>A registered landmark rule with a border-matched control.</strong> E7 leaves the alignment borders in place. It also needs person-clustered intervals for the descriptive falls: SelfMAD AMSL and WebMorph, and the recipes' collapses (M1, M5, M6, M8, M8O).</li>
<li><strong>Per-network registered rules for the recipes, with a negative control.</strong> None has been applied so far.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>B and C4 (benchmark construction)</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Hash the 15,518 enumerated files. They were listed, not hashed; a hash-only manifest is proposed.</li>
<li>C4's grade is final; X6 could only support a separately registered causal claim. A storage-format sentence would need a new registered claim (no row read a storage-format cue, H-new-142).</li>
</ul></div>
</section>
<section class="s" id="s5">
<h2><span class="n">5</span>What this does not show</h2>
<div class="prose"><ul>
<li>
<p><strong>It does not grade the methods.</strong> The verdict words are post hoc descriptive labels. The only grades are CONCLUSIONS':</p>
<ul>
<li>C1 "strong (final)";</li>
<li>C2 "descriptive row (final)";</li>
<li>C3 "strong (narrow)";</li>
<li>B "meets its registered criterion";</li>
<li>C4 "construction sentence".</li>
</ul>
<p>The regular (landmark) column has no shortcut grade at all.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>The release results are not about the methods.</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Each release is one network. C1 and C2 are about those checkpoints, not about the SelfMAD or MADation methods.</li>
<li>Never write "published detectors rely on shortcuts", "for every method" or "MADation carries the shortcut".</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Most results are on our frames, not the authors' pipelines.</strong><ul>
<li>The releases were scored on our 512-px eye-aligned frames. Only the SelfMAD release was also scored with its own crop and on raw files, and its raw-file E7 covers three diffusion families only.</li>
<li>Several release numbers do not reproduce on our frames (§3).</li>
<li>The MixFaceNet-MAD release is out of its input domain on our frames.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>The recipes are re-implementations that mostly did not reproduce.</strong><ul>
<li>Most CNN recipes failed the reproduction check.</li>
<li>Their benchmark pool is an eval-pipeline diagnostic (A12.4).</li>
<li>A12 numbers must not be set against published tables in the paper.</li>
<li>The same architectures trained with our protocol and data (P × d1) are not competing methods. They are in <code>gather_regular.md</code> §5 and <code>gather_diffusion.md</code> §3e.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>The data are narrow.</strong><ul>
<li>FRLL and MAD22 share one pool of 204 or 185 genuine photos from 102 persons, so the families are not independent.</li>
<li>The controlled Poisson set is CASIA-WebFace at 112 px.</li>
<li>GAN evidence is one generator per test: StyleGAN3 for H3 and X4, StyleGAN v1 for H4b.</li>
<li>The generator-matched diffusion evidence is DiffAE (X3, releases only, with a failed positive control). H2 and Arc2Face are other generators.</li>
<li>FFHQ-Morphs is left out of the verdicts. File size alone separates its DiffAE pool (1.000), and its own README calls it "unfit to be used for evaluation purposes".</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>It does not show which cue a network reads, or anything causal.</strong><ul>
<li>SelfMAD release: "cue not identified". Never write "resolution", "aliasing", "fine-detail loss", "interpolation trace" or "spectral cue".</li>
<li>GANs: no row reached the registered "storage-format cue" label (H-new-142).</li>
<li>MixFaceNet-MAD: no image-size sentence.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>It does not show that detectors are blind to identity.</strong><ul>
<li>H3's classes differ in latent spread by construction, and X4 has little power against a two-identity signal by construction.</li>
<li>H2 is low-potency.</li>
<li>X3's positive control failed (0.636 ≤ 0.65): "assay sensitivity not shown".</li>
<li>Never write "does not separate", "no signal" or "no network" for C3.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Control-pool artefacts are not excluded.</strong><ul>
<li>E7 leaves the alignment borders on landmark families (E07 README).</li>
<li>A border or alignment artefact of our E7 pool is not formally excluded: the border test ran but could not be evaluated.</li>
<li>The MorDIFF E7 pool is the weaker control. Almost half (0.471) of that pool's difference from the native photos sits in the warped border (H-new-152 W-c). That is a pool property, not a border-test reading of any network. The pool also raises the ViT-L/14 negative control by +10.7.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>The reruns are not replications.</strong> The crop test and the kernel ladder re-use the same persons and images.</li>
<li><strong>It says nothing about MADMamba, W3, E10 or the FST.</strong><ul>
<li>CONCLUSIONS requires three things printed beside every shortcut claim: "MADMamba not evaluated on H3 (sealed)", the positive controls, and our own networks' history with the same control.</li>
<li>That history concerns our own networks, so it is not repeated here. Take it from CONCLUSIONS when writing the paper.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>It makes no generalisation claim.</strong> Nothing here supports a "GAN or diffusion generalisation" sentence for any method.</li>
</ul></div><div class="prose"><h3>Flags for the author</h3>
<ol>
<li><strong>C4 wording.</strong><ul>
<li>A41 DC-A (<code>a41_files.json</code>) records the FRLL StyleGAN morphs as JPEG (quality 74.7, 1024 px), not PNG. MAD22 MIPGAN-I/II are PNG against JPEG.</li>
<li>The G-test's file containers are not in DC-A, so "(PNG) … (JPEG)" is unlogged for the G-test, not false.</li>
<li>Your sign-off can only weaken the wording. A possible version: "generator output (PNG, or JPEG q≈75 for FRLL StyleGAN) with camera photos (JPEG)".</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>H1 primary variant.</strong><ul>
<li>This note uses <code>orig</code> as primary, as both A27 (PROTOCOL l.7751) and the A36 amendment 1 rule (l.9929) register it. <code>pp</code> is printed beside it.</li>
<li>The "claims on pp" phrase at l.8649 belongs to the A30 registration amendment 2 and covers A30's own networks only.</li>
<li>Three releases change label between <code>orig</code> and <code>pp</code>:<ul>
<li>ViT-B/16: 0.770 above / 0.727 not above.</li>
<li>ViT-L/14: 0.718 above / 0.684 not above.</li>
<li>FE ViT-B/16: 0.681 above / 0.681 not above.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Diffusion verdicts exclude X3.</strong> If X3 were counted as a same-generator control:</p>
<ul>
<li>FE ViT-L/14 would meet the old "works" rule (E7 kept on Greedy-DiM-S; X3 MorDIFF 0.742).</li>
<li>The ViT-B/16 release would be "partly" on X3 MorDIFF 0.847 alone.</li>
</ul>
<p>We left X3 out because its positive control failed and C2 calls the MorDIFF separation "open".</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Verdict cut-offs are post hoc.</strong> The cut-offs (10% on two families, 3 points, 5 points and 25% for diffusion) were set after seeing the numbers. Different cut-offs would move a few labels.</p>
</li>
<li><strong>Border evidence.</strong> Per-family border results are only in E07 <code>raw/data-shortcut.md</code>, which is outside this note's allowed sources. That file says border fraction alone separates FRLL FaceMorpher / OpenCV / WebMorph morphs from E7 photos at AUC 1.00 and MAD22 landmark morphs at 0.69, and that AMSL is not in that check. Only the README's general statement is used above.</li>
<li><strong>Mixed CI levels.</strong> Keep the level next to each number:<ul>
<li>A41 S2 / S3 / DC-8: person-clustered 95%.</li>
<li>A41 H3 bounds: one-sided 95% pair.</li>
<li>X4: two-sided pair 95%.</li>
<li>A36 H1 and H3: pair 90%.</li>
<li>A36 RSX: image bootstrap 95%.</li>
